\title{Composition Order as a Sufficient Statistic:\\
Exact Cell Measures, Slope Laws, and the Boundary of $\kappa$-Sufficiency}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{4 September 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
Let $B$ be a finite base of distinct primes. For an integer $v$, the \emph{cell} of $v$
relative to $B$ is $\cell(v)=\{p\in B: p\mid v\}$ and its \emph{composition order} is
$\kappa(v)=|\cell(v)|$. Empirical work on smooth-number production reports a graded law
$\log(\text{rate})\approx \text{dial}-\beta\kappa$ with a scale-stable slope
$\beta\approx 0.35$ across bit-widths $72$, $96$ and $128$, together with the finding that
$\kappa$ summarises the whole cell at $72$ and $96$ bits but not at $128$.

We give an exact account of what those findings measure. Over one period
$M=\prod_{p\in B}p$ the number of residues with cell exactly $S$ is
$\prod_{p\in B\setminus S}(p-1)$, so the small-prime divisibility events are \emph{exactly}
independent Bernoulli variables with biases $1/p$; and for every window length $N$ the cell
count deviates from its predicted value by at most $2^{|B\setminus S|}$, a bound uniform in
$N$. On this measure we analyse the additive model $\Lambda(S)=D-\sum_{p\in S}w_p$ and prove:
a sufficiency dichotomy ($\kappa$ sufficient $\iff$ $w$ constant, with failure already at
$\kappa=1$); an identification theorem for the graded law; a slope law making the fitted
slope the $q(1-q)$-weighted mean of $-w$, whence cross-scale slope stability is
\emph{equivalent} to weight homogeneity rather than independent evidence; a closed form
$\mathcal R=\bigl(\tfrac12\sum_{p,r}v_pv_r(w_p-w_r)^2\bigr)/\sum_p v_p$ for the identity
increment, vanishing exactly when $\kappa$ is sufficient, with a sharp Popoviciu bound
converting a measured increment into a certified lower bound on the weight spread; an
orthogonality theorem giving $\Var\Lambda=\beta^2\Var\kappa+\mathcal R$; and a boundary
calculus localising the observed sufficiency crossing strictly inside $(96,128]$ while
showing that the $72$-bit verdict is forced by the $96$-bit one. Four experimental questions
thereby collapse into one question about a single hidden object, the weight profile $w$.
\end{abstract}

